% The impact analysis takes into consideration disaggregated banks' portfolio characteristics in terms of exposure types (households,non-financial corporations, financial corporations other than credit institutions) as well as  the geographic composition   of  these exposures (worldwide-country of destiny) at bank level. Also  the intensity of cross-border diversification and the regulatory status under the Single Supervisory Mechanism (SSM) are  analysed as potential determinants of non-performing loans. Interestingly, the quantile regression approach also allows a disaggregated estimation of the determinants  along the conditional distribution of non-performing loans. The results shows the importance of bank-specific ratios and  the characteristics of the legal system together with macroeconomic indicators on explaining non-performing loans. Regulation and Supervision under the Single Supervisory Mechanism  appears to be significant and  negatively correlated with NPLs with similar effects along the conditional NPL distribution while the effects of geographic diversification are inconclusive. Regarding the disaggregated loan-portfolio, each exposure types showed similar effects of NPL-determinants in terms of the direction of the impact (+/-) but differences in intensity and explanatory power, with the greatest impact being shown by non-financial corporations followed by households and financial corporations. Additionally, the marginal impact of the NPL-determinants tend to increase along the conditional NPL-distribution for all portfolio types in a similar pace, showing that the intensity of the impact increases  for banks with higher non-performing ratios. These results together with the data and methodologies used in this paper provides interesting insights with potential practical implications for EU-policy making, recommendations on NPL strategies, the design of Early Warning Indicators (EWI) and bank-stress testing methodologies.

% therefore, the main purpose of this thesis is to contribute to a further understanding  of the determinants of  NPLs by  analyzing the impact of macroeconomic and bank-specific factors on Non-Performing Loans  in the EU-banking sector using a  granular dataset on major European banks gathered by the European Banking Authority (EBA) in the context of the EU-wide Transparency Exercise for the period 2014-2019. Motivated by the granularity of this dataset,  important aspects of a disaggregated banks' portfolio, such as different exposure types, geographic composition   of  NPL portfolios, intensity of cross-border diversification or the regulatory status under the Single Supervisory Mechanism (SSM)  will also be given special focus. The use of the EBA data in this thesis is even more  relevant as there are rarely any academic paper  in the Non-Performing Loans (NPL) literature that may any use or reference to the EBA transparency exercises Database despite its high quality standards, the novelty of its compilation and its usefulness to handle some of the difficulties presented in the NPL literature. More details about its characteristics, advantages and the institutions behind its compilation  will  also be provided. For the empirical analysis, quantile regression models for panel data  will also be  estimated. These methods provide new insights into  the determinants of  NPLs by  allowing a disaggregated estimation of their  impact at different NPL levels along the conditional distribution of NPLs in the EU banking sector. 


