% The objective of this paper is to analyze  the impact of macroeconomic and bank-specific factors on Non-Performing Loans (NPL) in the EU-banking sector using an extensive, granular dataset on major European banks gathered by the European Banking Authority (EBA) in the context of the EU-wide Transparency Exercise for the period 2014-2019. The impact analysis takes into consideration disaggregated banks' portfolio characteristics in terms of exposure types (households,non-financial corporations, financial corporations other than credit institutions) as well as  the geographic composition   of  these exposures (worldwide-country of destiny) at bank level. Also  the intensity of cross-border diversification and the regulatory status under the Single Supervisory Mechanism (SSM) are  analysed as potential determinants of non-performing loans. For the empirical approach, traditional panel data methodologies (Random Effects/Fixed Effects/GMM) and quantile regression methods for panel data (Pooled/Penalixed Fixed Effects) have been applied. Interestingly, the quantile regression approach also allows a disaggregated estimation of the determinants  along the conditional distribution of non-performing loans. The results shows the importance of bank-specific ratios and  the characteristics of the legal system together with macroeconomic indicators on explaining non-performing loans. Regulation and Supervision under the Single Supervisory Mechanism  appears to be significant and  negatively correlated with NPLs with similar effects along the conditional NPL distribution while the effects of geographic diversification are inconclusive. Regarding the disaggregated loan-portfolio, each exposure types showed similar effects of NPL-determinants in terms of the direction of the impact (+/-) but differences in intensity and explanatory power, with the greatest impact being shown by non-financial corporations followed by households and financial corporations. Additionally, the marginal impact of the NPL-determinants tend to increase along the conditional NPL-distribution for all portfolio types in a similar pace, showing that the intensity of the impact increases  for banks with higher non-performing ratios. These results together with the data and methodologies used in this paper provides interesting insights with potential practical implications for EU-policy making, recommendations on NPL strategies, the design of Early Warning Indicators (EWI) and bank-stress testing methodologies.

